# LINE ENDINGS ARE LF, ON EVERY MACHINE.
#
# Git on Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout by default (core.autocrlf),
# which is a sensible default for a repository of Windows text files and the
# wrong one here. This project reads its own source as TEXT: the test harness
# lifts functions out of .gs files with regular expressions, and the suites
# assert on exact strings in them. A regex looking for "\n}" does not match
# "\r\n}", so a checkout on Windows failed knowledge-test with "could not lift
# learnKind" - a message that says nothing at all about line endings, on a
# machine where nothing was wrong except how git wrote the file out.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: decide for yourself what is text, and whatever you
# decide, write it out with LF. The working copy then matches what is stored,
# on Windows, macOS and Linux alike, and Apps Script does not care either way.
#
# If a checkout already has CRLF in it, this file alone does not fix it -
# git has to be told to write the files again:
#
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git checkout -- .
#
# wiring-test refuses a carriage return anywhere in src/, so a file that slips
# through fails the suite rather than one regex somewhere.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Not text, and must never be rewritten.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.docx binary
*.zip binary
*.mp4 binary
